#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332 :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048754 :topic/math-academy-id 312 :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628 :topic/math-academy-id 334 :topic/title "Integrating Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] :topic/id #uuid "2d31fc2e-19fe-50d4-bccf-d60b3f5db5c4" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096454 :question/id #uuid "9a42787b-24fb-50c8-9f3f-9e95d35c403c" :question/math-academy-id "e-459" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int e^{1-3x}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=1-3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{1-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{1-3x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096455 :question/id #uuid "cbf09123-3650-5d6a-869a-8edd8f54206b" :question/math-academy-id "e-460" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int 3^{5+4x}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=5+4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 4\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 4 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {3}^{5+4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {3}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{5+4x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a General Exponential Function With a Linear Exponent"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1112 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048245 :topic/math-academy-id 3770 :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Substitution"}] :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Linear Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332 :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution"}] :topic/id #uuid "4d6f30e9-3dde-50ef-bfb2-830878d94eaf" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068915 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068567 :question/id #uuid "0021ae6a-8ef8-564e-9c2e-fb5f9e8f7313" :question/math-academy-id "e-464" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 2 \\sin {\\left(\\dfrac x 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\dfrac x 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068568 :question/id #uuid "daf5cc74-4c7d-500a-9345-03bd6b73a333" :question/math-academy-id "e-465" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 5 \\csc {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\cot {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 6\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =\\dfrac 1 6\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068569 :question/id #uuid "234c7a86-037c-5316-9419-14db90d1da60" :question/math-academy-id "e-466" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sec^2\\left(x^3-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068570 :question/id #uuid "03e461a3-0fe0-5fb5-813e-ae23a5dc606a" :question/math-academy-id "e-467" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 478 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048528 :topic/math-academy-id 3257 :topic/title "Integrating Hyperbolic Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048878 :topic/math-academy-id 3260 :topic/title "Integration Using Basic Hyperbolic Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049202 :topic/math-academy-id 1085 :topic/title "Integrating Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049280 :topic/math-academy-id 1203 :topic/title "The Average Value of a Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380 :topic/math-academy-id 1061 :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Direct Integration"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049947 :topic/math-academy-id 762 :topic/title "Calculating Velocity Using Integration"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207 :topic/math-academy-id 1992 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Rectangular Domains"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050217 :topic/math-academy-id 760 :topic/title "The Arc Length of a Planar Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050379 :topic/math-academy-id 759 :topic/title "Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050783 :topic/math-academy-id 282 :topic/title "Integration Using Basic Trigonometric Identities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929 :topic/math-academy-id 317 :topic/title "Introduction to Integration by Parts"}] :topic/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321 :topic/math-academy-id 3769 :topic/title "The Sum Rule for Indefinite Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/math-academy-id 1108 :topic/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "5a0d0fb8-c0a3-55dc-a497-9f0d5f0eda76" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064203 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064071 :question/id #uuid "eff323d1-4e70-5490-8e41-9fbf00c387f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-446" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int (3x+1)^3 \\,  \\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{12}(3x+1)^{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(3x+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 4(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x+1)^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(3x+1)^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Positive Integer Exponent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064204 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064072 :question/id #uuid "e469c042-9619-55d4-b745-d4ed26251d80" :question/math-academy-id "e-447" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2}{(1-x)^3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2(1-x)^{-3} \\,  \\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =  -\\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2(1-x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{u}^{-3}\\cdot (-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\int {u}^{-3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{-2}}{-2}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =(1-x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-2(1-x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064075 :question/id #uuid "e416cb5c-22df-55e4-a3cb-c6b5dfc650c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-448" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int\\sqrt{1-5x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = -\\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-5x)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}(1-5x)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(1-5x)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-5x)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-5x)^{1/2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(1-5x)^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :topic/math-academy-id 334 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557 :topic/math-academy-id 331 :topic/title "Integrating Linear Rational Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101 :topic/math-academy-id 1112 :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Linear Substitution"}] :topic/title "Integrating Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050985 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842 :topic/math-academy-id 1110 :topic/title "The Quotient Rule for Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "770e795e-38d4-5b4f-8bd2-bd698bb6794a" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115019 :question/id #uuid "118a33c0-5831-586e-a0a5-e8bd3e1e4c9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3644" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2 \\csc x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =-2csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115020 :question/id #uuid "23b1cfcf-9f46-5274-b07b-66770a827409" :question/math-academy-id "e-3646" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac {\\textrm d y} {\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 2x - 3\\cot x.$" :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3cot⁡x) \\\\  & =2-3(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2+3{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115021 :question/id #uuid "9b2548d6-87fc-56b1-aa7d-cef328eb4e24" :question/math-academy-id "e-3647" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = x\\sec x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xsec⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot sec⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sec⁡x) \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot sec⁡x+x\\cdot (sec⁡xtan⁡x) \\\\  & =sec⁡x(1+xtan⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115022 :question/id #uuid "83b8a6dd-c8c4-5537-ac88-289265752755" :question/math-academy-id "e-3648" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ if $f(x) = \\dfrac{-2\\cot x}{\\sin x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{-2cot⁡x}{sin⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2cot⁡x)\\cdot sin⁡x-(-2cot⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)}{{[sin⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot sin⁡x+2cot⁡x\\cdot (cos⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2csc⁡x+2cot⁡xcos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{2csc⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2cot⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})+2\\cdot (1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1686 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777 :topic/math-academy-id 285 :topic/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050643 :topic/math-academy-id 637 :topic/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789 :topic/math-academy-id 305 :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/title "Differentiating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}]}
